---
import PuzzleFrame from '../../components/PuzzleFrame.astro';
import concepts from '../../data/puzzle-concepts.json';
import research from '../../data/puzzle-research.json';
const title = 'A world, piece by piece';
const description = 'Jigsaw puzzles, ten original visual concepts, a gentle playable picture puzzle, resources for making and buying, and an open business and design studio.';
const categories = [...new Set(concepts.map(c=>c.category))];
---
<PuzzleFrame title={title} description={description} active="world">
  <header class="p-hero"><div class="p-hero-copy"><p class="eyebrow">POINTCAST PUZZLE WORLD · START WITH JIGSAWS</p><h1>A world,<br/>piece by<br/>piece.</h1><p class="p-lead">A little attention. A table to gather around. An image you come to know with your hands.</p><p>Begin with jigsaws: why we return to them, what makes a good one, where to find one, and how we could make our own.</p><div class="p-actions"><a class="p-button" href="#concepts">Explore ten worlds ↓</a><a class="p-button secondary" href="#play">Try a little puzzle</a></div><div class="p-stats"><div><strong>10</strong><span>ORIGINAL ART CONCEPTS</span></div><div><strong>300–1,500</strong><span>PROPOSED PIECE COUNTS</span></div><div><strong>Your pace</strong><span>NO TIMER</span></div></div></div><figure class="p-hero-art"><img src="/images/puzzles/01-tidepool-atlas.webp" alt={concepts[0].alt} width="1536" height="1024" fetchpriority="high"/><figcaption>01 / Tidepool atlas · Original AI-generated artwork study · Proposed 500-piece puzzle</figcaption></figure></header>
  <div class="p-band">An idea shelf, a making guide, and a learning studio. PointCast physical puzzle concepts are not yet for sale.</div>
  <section class="p-section" id="why"><div class="p-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">01 · THE PLEASURE OF A SMALL FINISH</p><h2>Why do we<br/>like this so much?</h2></div><p class="p-lead">A jigsaw gives attention somewhere to land. You can work alone, alongside someone, or leave it on the table and return tomorrow.</p></div><div class="p-grid"><article class="p-card"><h3>Progress you can touch</h3><p>A loose piece becomes part of a larger picture. Small, visible finishes can make a long task feel inviting.</p><p class="p-small">An editorial interpretation of the activity, not a measured promise about wellbeing.</p></article><article class="p-card"><h3>A shared table</h3><p>People can build separate sections, trade observations and contribute without having to keep a conversation going.</p><p class="p-small">Choose an image with several landmarks, and ask each person what a comfortable challenge looks like.</p></article><article class="p-card"><h3>Looking becomes knowing</h3><p>You study color, shape, orientation and texture in a way a quick glance never asks of you.</p><p class="p-small">A 2018 randomized study found improved puzzle skill but no between-group improvement in overall visuospatial cognition after 30 days. It included healthy adults aged 50+ and was funded by a puzzle maker. <a href="https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/aging-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnagi.2018.00299/full" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the study ↗</a></p></article></div><p class="p-note" style="margin-top:24px">Enjoyment is enough. Puzzles are recreation; this feature makes no claims about treating anxiety, preventing dementia or healing through sound.</p></section>
  <section class="p-section" id="start"><div class="p-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">02 · FIND YOUR KIND OF CHALLENGE</p><h2>Piece count is<br/>only one dial.</h2></div><p>Image complexity, piece size, cut variety, contrast, eyesight, dexterity, table space and time all matter. A dark 500-piece image can be harder than a bright 1,000-piece scene.</p></div><div class="p-grid"><article class="p-card"><p class="eyebrow">A GENTLE START</p><h3>100–300 pieces</h3><p>Try distinct objects and strong color zones. Larger pieces may be easier to handle; compare physical dimensions, not just the count.</p></article><article class="p-card"><p class="eyebrow">A TABLE FOR A WHILE</p><h3>500–1,000 pieces</h3><p>Enough room for texture and a longer session. Pick a subject you actually want to look at, and check the finished size before opening the box.</p></article><article class="p-card"><p class="eyebrow">A LONGER PROJECT</p><h3>1,500+ pieces</h3><p>More space and sorting. Dense architecture can offer landmarks; monochrome expanses or repeating patterns add challenge at any count.</p></article></div><details><summary>Make the table welcoming</summary><p>Use even light with low glare, a contrasting mat, a large reference image and sorting trays within easy reach. Let people stand, sit, stop or ask for a hint. Offer large-piece alternatives and image landmarks that do not depend on color alone. These are design suggestions; test them with the people who will use the puzzle.</p></details><details><summary>A small history, and more ways to puzzle</summary><p>London engraver and map seller John Spilsbury is widely credited with early jigsaw puzzles. The National Portrait Gallery dates his puzzle to 1766. Dissected maps used pictures as teaching tools; today’s image puzzles turn that same idea into many kinds of leisure. <a href="https://www.npg.org.uk/collections/search/person/mp62395/john-spilsbury" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">National Portrait Gallery history ↗</a></p><p>Today’s world includes cardboard and laser-cut wood, irregular cuts, extra-large pieces, round images, shaped puzzles, mystery scenes and three-dimensional constructions. This first PointCast edition concentrates on two-dimensional image puzzles. Crosswords, logic, mechanical puzzles and other forms can become future rooms.</p></details><details><summary>Your first session: a five-step guide</summary><ol><li>Check the size, clear a surface, and keep the box reference nearby.</li><li>Turn pieces image-side up; find edges if the cut has conventional edges.</li><li>Sort a few obvious zones rather than creating twenty tiny piles.</li><li>Build a recognizable object; rotate candidates gently and never force a fit.</li><li>Pause whenever you like. Store the pieces safely and return at your pace.</li></ol></details></section>
  <section class="p-section" id="concepts"><div class="p-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">03 · TEN ORIGINAL WORLDS</p><h2>What could<br/>PointCast make?</h2></div><div><p class="p-lead">A coast, a greenhouse, an impossible terminal. Ten different reasons to sit down and look closely.</p><p>These are original AI-generated concept images, with proposed piece plans. No factory samples, final dies or inventory exist. Web images are 1,536 × 1,024; they are concept previews, not production print masters.</p></div></div><form class="p-filter" id="p-filter"><label>Choose a world<select id="p-category"><option value="all">All worlds</option>{categories.map(c=><option value={c}>{c}</option>)}</select></label><label>Proposed piece count<select id="p-count"><option value="all">Any count</option><option value="gentle">300–500</option><option value="middle">750–1,000</option><option value="advanced">1,500</option></select></label><button class="p-button secondary" type="reset">Show all</button><p id="p-filter-status" role="status" aria-live="polite">10 concepts</p></form><div class="p-concepts">{concepts.map(c=><article class="p-concept" id={`concept-${c.slug}`} data-concept data-category={c.category} data-pieces={c.pieces}><img src={`/images/puzzles/${c.art}`} alt={c.alt} width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy"/><div class="p-concept-body"><div class="p-concept-meta"><span>{c.id} / {c.category.toUpperCase()}</span><span>CONCEPT · NOT FOR SALE</span></div><h3>{c.title}</h3><p>{c.story}</p><dl><dt>For</dt><dd>{c.audience}</dd><dt>Piece plan</dt><dd>{c.pieces.toLocaleString()} pieces · {c.format}</dd><dt>Solving</dt><dd>{c.challenge}</dd></dl><details><summary>Print study & business question</summary><p><b>Print:</b> {c.print}</p><p><b>First test:</b> {c.pilot}</p><p><b>Design lesson:</b> {c.lesson}</p><p class="p-small">Final crop, resolution, tolerances, piece dimensions and cut must follow the selected supplier’s specification and an approved physical proof.</p></details><a href={`/puzzles/studio/#brief-${c.id}`}>Study this concept →</a></div></article>)}</div><noscript><p>All ten concepts are visible. Filtering requires JavaScript.</p></noscript></section>
  <section class="p-section" id="play"><div class="p-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">04 · A SMALL PRACTICE TABLE</p><h2>Try the tidepool.</h2></div><p>A 12-piece picture assembly demo using our original art. These digital pieces have straight cuts; physical jigsaws will need tested interlocking dies. There is no timer or score to chase.</p></div><div id="p-demo"><div class="p-demo-grid"><div><div class="p-board" id="p-board" aria-label="Puzzle board, three rows and four columns"></div><p class="p-demo-status" id="p-demo-status" role="status" aria-live="polite">Enable JavaScript to play this picture assembly demo.</p></div><div><h3>Pick a piece.<br/>Pick a place.</h3><p>Select a piece below, then choose a board space. Use Tab to move between buttons, Enter or Space to select. Wrong placements give a hint and keep your piece selected.</p><label><input type="checkbox" id="p-piece-hints"/> Show each piece’s target row and column</label><div class="p-actions"><button type="button" id="p-reset" class="p-button" disabled>Start again</button><button type="button" id="p-show-guide" class="p-button secondary" aria-expanded="false">Show picture guide</button></div><img id="p-guide" class="p-guide-img" hidden src="/images/puzzles/01-tidepool-atlas.webp" alt="Full Tidepool atlas image to use as an assembly guide" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy"/></div></div><div class="p-tray" id="p-tray" aria-label="Available puzzle pieces"></div></div></section>
  <section class="p-section" id="soundtrack"><div class="p-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">05 · OPTIONAL SOUNDTRACK</p><h2>Leave a little<br/>room to drift.</h2></div><div><p class="p-lead">Mike’s playlist: <i>veridis quo surfer dreams.</i></p><p>The official Spotify page identifies this playlist and creator mhoydich. We have not listened to or reviewed the audio; music is optional.</p><a class="p-button" href="https://open.spotify.com/playlist/0AegDPw3ddtdk31PHRB1Og?si=7d8cba49633940a6" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open the playlist on Spotify ↗</a><p class="p-small" style="margin-top:15px">Opens the official service. No audio, lyrics or cover art are hosted here. No claims are made about healing frequencies.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="p-section"><div class="p-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">06 · THE NEXT THREE ROOMS</p><h2>Buy one.<br/>Make one. Learn.</h2></div><p>A useful world includes the pleasure of the object and the reality of making it. Follow the path that interests you.</p></div><div class="p-grid"><a class="p-card" href="/shop/puzzles/"><h3>The puzzle shelf ↗</h3><p>Official publisher links, choosing guidance and no invented stock or checkout.</p></a><a class="p-card" href="/puzzles/make/"><h3>The making guide →</h3><p>Art, print, mounting, cut, proof, packaging, rights and safety. The process behind the table.</p></a><a class="p-card" href="/puzzles/business/"><h3>The business lab →</h3><p>Five routes to market, realistic cost assumptions and an interactive contribution calculator.</p></a></div></section>
  <section class="p-section" id="sources"><div class="p-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">07 · SOURCE NOTES & LIMITS</p><h2>Keep the<br/>evidence visible.</h2></div><p>Sources checked October 3, 2026. Prices, policies and catalogs can change. Manufacturer claims are labeled as such. Original illustrations depict invented places and people; they do not document an actual project.</p></div><ol class="p-source-list">{research.sources.map(s=><li><a href={s.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{s.title} ↗</a><br/>{s.note}<br/><span class="p-source-date">Checked {s.accessed}</span></li>)}</ol><p class="p-note">AI generation does not establish exclusive copyright or guarantee freedom from third-party claims. Before a commercial launch: preserve prompts and source history, document human edits, inspect artwork, secure needed permissions and review the production master. <a href="/puzzles/make/#rights">Read the rights workflow</a>.</p></section>
</PuzzleFrame>
<script>
  import { setupPuzzleWorld } from '../../lib/puzzle-world-client';
  setupPuzzleWorld();
  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load',setupPuzzleWorld);
</script>
